What are ACL's :

	- Access Control List have multiple uses

	- Function as a packet filter, Tell the router to permit or discard specific traffic

	- ACL's filter traffic based on

		# Source / Destination Ip address

		# Source / Destination Layer 4 Ports 

	- How Access Control List work

		* ACL's are configured globaly on the router ( Global Config Mode )

		* They are an ordered sequence of ACEs ( Access Control Entries )

			ex ACL 1 :

				  # If source Ip = 192.168.1.0/24, then permit

				  # If source IP = 192.168.2.0/24, then deny

				  # If source Ip = any, then permit

		* Configured an ACL in Global config mode will not make the ACL Take effect

			- The ACL must be applied to an interface
		
			- ACLs are applied either inbound or outbound

		* ACLs are made up of one or more ACEs

		* When the router checks a packet against the ACL, it processes the ACEs in order

			Order :

				- Top

				- Bottom

			- if the packets mathces one of the ACE in the ACL, the router take action,
			  and stops proccessing the ACL.

				- Entries below the matching will be ignored

				ex  ACL 2:

					# If source IP = 192.168.1.0/24, then permit 

					# If source IP = 192.168.0.0/16 then deny ( Ignrored if top match )

				* A maximum of One ACL can be applied to a single interface per direction

					# Inbound 

					# Outbound

		* Implicit Deny :

			- Comes into play when the packet doesn't match any entry in the ACL

			- Tell the router to deny all traffic that doesnt match any configured entries in

			  the ACL

Access Control List Types :

	- Standard Access Control List : Match based on Source IP Address only

		-> Standard Numbered Access Control List

		-> Standard Name Access Control List

	- Extended Access Control List : Match based on Source / Destination IP, Source / Destination Port

		-> Extended Numbered Access Control List

		-> Extended Named Access Control List

	* Standard Numbered (ACLs)

		- Match Traffic based only on the source IP address of the packet
 
		- Numbered ACL's are identified with a number ( ei. ACL 1, ACl 2, etc)

		- Different types of ACLs have a different range of numbers that can be used
			 
			Protocol 		      Range

			Standard IP  		1-99 and 1300-1999

		- Standard ACL should be applied as close to the destination as possible

	* Standard Named (ACLs)

		- Match traffic based only on the source IP address of the packet

		- Named ACLs are identified with a name (ie. 'Block_BOB')

		- Standard named ACLs are configured by entering 'standard named ACL config mode', and then
		  configuring each entry within that config mode
		
					



			

		



















